   "subject": "Re: [geomorph-r-package] Re: TEST OF HOMOGENEITY OF SLOPES (ALLOMETRY)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\">Nicole,<div><br></div><div>To expand on Dean’s reply, a ~ Csize + species model would be a common slopes model allowing for different group intercepts (means) and a ~ Csize model would be both a common slope and common intercept model.  Your full model, ~ Csize * species, will find unique intercepts and slopes for species.  Therefore, you might want to think about exactly what the hypothesis is that you wish to test and what the best null model should be.  The HOS test in procD.allmoetry used the former null model (different intercepts, common slope), so if you are trying to mimic that, you probably should use that as a null model in advanced.procD.lm.</div><div><br></div><div>Mike<br><div><br><blockquote type=\"cite\"></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On Feb 22, 2019, at 9:15 AM, Adams, Dean [EEOBS] &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">dca...@iastate.edu</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div style=\"font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px;font-style:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:start;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;text-decoration:none\"><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Nicole,<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\"><u></u> <u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">As specified in your code yes. More technically, the function performs pairwise comparisons from the full model, relative to the reduced model, where both models are specified by the user. You have specified Csize*species as the full model, and Csize as your reduced. Using just the covariate (Csize) as the reduced model is equivalent to a common slopes model (i.e. parallel regression lines).<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\"><u></u> <u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Dean<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\"><u></u> <u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Dr. Dean C. Adams<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Director of Graduate Education, EEB Program<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Professor<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Department of Ecology, Evolution, and Organismal Biology<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">Iowa State University<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><a href=\"https://www.eeob.iastate.edu/faculty/adams/\" style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.eeob.iastate.edu/faculty/adams/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792485117294000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3suPOrgxCmISA1rHV6HYEL\">https://www.eeob.iastate.edu/faculty/adams/</a><u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\">phone: <a href=\"tel:(515)%20294-3834\" value=\"+15152943834\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">515-294-3834</a><u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif;color:rgb(31,73,125)\"><u></u> <u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\">From:</span></b><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif\"><span> </span><a href style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a><span> </span>&lt;<a href style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt;<span> </span><b>On Behalf Of<span> </span></b>Nicole Torres Tamayo<br><b>Sent:</b><span> </span>Friday, February 22, 2019 4:38 AM<br><b>To:</b><span> </span><a href style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a><br><b>Subject:</b><span> </span>[geomorph-r-package] Re: TEST OF HOMOGENEITY OF SLOPES (ALLOMETRY)<u></u><u></u></span></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div><div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Hi Dean<u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Thank you very much for your reply. Just one more quick question: the null hypothesis associated to the p-values of the angles between slopes is that the slopes are paralell?<u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">All the best,<u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Nicole<u></u><u></u></div></div></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div><div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">El jue., 21 feb. 2019 a las 18:10, Nicole Torres Tamayo (&lt;<a href style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">nicole...@gmail.com</a>&gt;) escribió:<u></u><u></u></div></div><blockquote style=\"border-style:none none none solid;border-left-width:1pt;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);padding:0in 0in 0in 6pt;margin-left:4.8pt;margin-right:0in\"><div><div><div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Hi geomorph community :)<span> </span><u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">I have a dataset with 3 species and I want to test whether the allometry pattern is similar or different between them. For that, I did a test of homogeneity of slopes:<u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">reg &lt;- procD.allometry(coords~Csize,~species,groups=~species,slope=~Csize,iter=1999,logsz=FALSE, print.progress=FALSE,data=gdf_C1)<u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Here I obtain that my 3 species show different slopes (null hypothesis of parallel slopes rejected).<u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Then, I try to calculate the angle between these slopes using the following function:<u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">advanced.procD.lm(coords ~ Csize,~Csize*species,groups=~species,angle.type = &quot;deg&quot;,slope =~Csize, iter=999,data=gdf_C1)<u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">However, when I run this analysis, there is no result related to the angles between slopes vectors in my output (although there are other outputs such as slopes, effect sizes, etc) Am I missing something?<u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Thank you very much in advance.<u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">All the best,<u></u><u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><u></u> <u></u></div></div><div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">Nicole<u></u><u></u></div></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0.0001pt;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\">--<span> </span><br>You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;geomorph R package&quot; group.<br>To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to<span> </span><a href style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-r-pack...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>To post to this group, send email to<span> </span><a href style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">geomorph-...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>Visit this group at<span> </span><a href=\"https://groups.google.com/group/geomorph-r-package\" style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/group/geomorph-r-package&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792485117294000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1tWE0HQaYn5co6cmZlN0CN\">https://groups.google.com/group/geomorph-r-package</a>.<br>To view this discussion on the web, visit<span> </span><a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msgid/geomorph-r-package/CAD8ohKTiwt%2B2quAehVFbnh44Tk%2B6%2Bdf-RMFL_F9OGans5bM_vw%40mail.gmail.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer\" style=\"color:purple;text-decoration:underline\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-safer